\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,booktabs,hyperref}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}\newtheorem{proposition}[theorem]{Proposition}
\newcommand{\Aut}{\operatorname{Aut}}
\title{Quartic graphs with five pentagons through every edge}
\author{}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\noindent An edge-girth-regular graph with parameters $(n,k,g,\lambda)$ is a
simple $k$-regular graph of order $n$ and girth $g$ in which every edge lies
on exactly $\lambda$ cycles of length $g$. We recover the following entry
of the fixed source, Table~2 on physical page~18 \cite{source}.
\begin{theorem}\label{main}
The minimum order of an edge-girth-regular graph with $k=4$, $g=5$ and
$\lambda=5$ is $24$. There is exactly one isomorphism class at that order.
\end{theorem}
The numerical result and a representative, \texttt{UntilOrder250\_115},
belong to the source and its associated data. The proof below uses the
acquired normalization of matchings along a forest \cite{forest} and a
complete finite certificate. The vertex Moore ball is classical; its use
here also follows the previously acquired quartic girth-five component.
The changed part of the argument is the completion through a small
exterior, with exact pentagon counts maintained during construction.

\section*{The induced Moore ball}
Fix a vertex $r$ of a putative graph. Write $u_0,\ldots,u_3$ for its
neighbours and $X_i$ for the three neighbours of $u_i$ other than $r$.
Girth five implies that these seventeen vertices are distinct. Their
induced subgraph $R$ consists of the sixteen tree edges and edges between
different sets $X_i$. Indeed, $r$ and the $u_i$ already have degree four,
and an edge inside $X_i$ would give a triangle. The edges between $X_i$
and $X_j$ form a matching: two neighbours of one leaf in $X_j$ would give
a quadrangle through $u_j$.

Let $k_{ij}$ be the size of this matching. A pentagon through $ru_i$ uses
one cross edge from $X_i$ to another branch, and every such cross edge
gives one pentagon. Consequently
\[
 0\le k_{ij}\le3,\qquad \sum_{j\ne i}k_{ij}=5.
\]
Subtracting the sum of the equations for $i=2,3$ from those for $i=0,1$
gives $2(k_{01}-k_{23})=0$. The other two analogous differences give
\[
 k_{01}=k_{23}=a,\quad k_{02}=k_{13}=b,\quad k_{03}=k_{12}=c,
 \qquad a+b+c=5.
\]
The action of the four branch permutations induces all permutations of
these three opposite-edge pairs. Thus, up to branch permutation, the
possibilities are
\[
 (a,b,c)=(0,2,3),\quad(1,1,3),\quad(1,2,2).
\]
There are twelve labelled size vectors; the certificate checks this
integer inventory independently of the graph constructor.

Apply the acquired matching-forest theorem to the branch graph $K_4$,
with a three-element set at each vertex. Its permitted relabellings act
on the leaves and extend to actual permutations of the whole Moore ball.
They therefore preserve possible completions. For an oriented forest
edge with a matching of size $s>0$, choose its $s$ parent labels and send
them in increasing order to the first $s$ child labels. There are
$\binom3s$ choices. Away from the forest, enumerate all
$\binom3s^2s!$ partial bijections. The acquired theorem proves that this
normalization retains every orbit; we do not infer coverage from the
resulting counts. The three products contain respectively $54$, $2187$
and $39366$ assignments. Partial degree and girth tests are necessary
conditions and hence may be imposed before reaching a full assignment.

The complete traversal of all $41607$ assignments leaves $15270$ induced
roots. Each is joined to one of $348$ stored roots by a checked literal
bijection preserving adjacency and the root, parent and leaf roles.
A canonical-labelling routine proposes the maps; every map is checked
on all adjacency entries. A mistaken proposal can cause failure, but
cannot certify an invalid identification.

\section*{The exterior}
The ten cross edges use twenty of the thirty-six leaf incidences left
after the parent edges. There are therefore exactly sixteen edges from
$R$ to its exterior $Y$. If $t=|Y|$ and $e=e(Y)$, degree counting gives
\begin{equation}\label{outside}
 4t=16+2e,\qquad e=2t-8.
\end{equation}
In particular the order is at least $21$. It remains to inspect
$(t,e)=(4,0),(5,2),(6,4),(7,6)$ to prove the asserted minimum and uniqueness.
The exterior is a simple graph $H$ of maximum degree at most four and
girth at least five. We enumerate every labelled edge subset of the
prescribed size, and again retain representatives only through literal
checked maps. The complete inventory is
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
t&4&5&6&7\\ \hline
e&0&2&4&6\\
\text{edge subsets}&1&45&1365&54264\\
\text{eligible labelled graphs}&1&45&1080&19782\\
\text{stored graphs}&1&2&6&12
\end{array}
\]
In particular the last inventory includes exteriors with cycles; no
assumption that the exterior is a forest is made.

Fix $R$ and $H$. If $h_y$ is the degree of $y$ in $H$, let $B_y$ be its
neighbours among the twelve leaves of $R$. Necessarily
\[
 |B_y|=4-h_y,\qquad |B_y\cap X_i|\le1.
\]
The second condition follows from the forbidden quadrangle through $u_i$.
Every remaining edge is specified by these subsets. Each leaf must occur
in exactly $4-\deg_R(x)$ of them. Once all subsets have been assigned,
there are no further unspecified vertices or edges.

We assign exterior vertices in decreasing order of $h_y$, breaking ties
by their labels. For each vertex we try every leaf subset of the required
size meeting each branch at most once. We reject a used-up leaf capacity,
or a remaining capacity greater than the number of unassigned exterior
vertices. Both tests are necessary. Before adding an edge, we reject it
if its endpoints already have a path of length at most three. Thus the
partial graph always has girth at least five.

\begin{lemma}\label{budget}
The exact number of pentagons on every present edge can be maintained
under an edge insertion using only the simple paths of length four
between the new endpoints in the previous graph. Rejecting a count
greater than five preserves every required completion.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every newly created pentagon contains the new edge $uv$. Removing that
edge gives a unique simple four-edge path from $u$ to $v$ in the previous
graph; conversely every such path creates one pentagon. The number of
paths is the count on $uv$. For each old edge, increment its count by the
number of these paths containing it. Initially the same path count gives
the exact count on every edge, so induction proves the assertion after
any sequence of insertions. Store the increments and reverse them on
backtracking. No count is estimated. Adding edges cannot destroy a
pentagon, which proves the safety of the rejection.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{symmetry}
One may retain only the lexicographically smallest vector $(B_y)_{y\in Y}$
in each orbit of $\Aut(H)$. The partial comparison used by the constructor
preserves every such minimum, irrespective of its vertex order.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
An automorphism of $H$, extended by the identity on $R$, preserves every
completion condition. Encode each leaf subset by its twelve-bit mask and
compare vectors in the fixed label order on $Y$. The finite orbit has a
unique minimum vector. To compare a partial vector with its image, scan
coordinates in that label order and stop as soon as either entry is
unassigned. If the preceding entries agree and the first assigned
difference is greater on the original side, no completion of that vector
can be the orbit minimum. All earlier comparisons are fixed forever.
Otherwise this comparison gives no rejection. Consequently every prefix
of a full orbit minimum survives. At a full vector the tests are exactly
lexicographic minimality. The code obtains $\Aut(H)$ by checking every
permutation of at most seven vertices, so there is no group-recognition
assumption. A separate assigned flag distinguishes an unassigned mask
from the legitimate empty subset.
\end{proof}

\section*{Complete finite certificate}
The constructor visits all $348$ retained roots and every exterior model.
At a leaf of the recursion it verifies degree four and exactly five
pentagons on every edge. The preceding arguments show that every graph
of order at most $24$ in the theorem has a retained representative.
Conversely these terminal tests establish all the required parameters.
Such a graph is connected: every quartic component of girth at least five
has at least seventeen vertices, whereas the total order is at most $24$.

\input{completion_table.tex}
The complete result has no graph at orders $21$, $22$ or $23$ and one
isomorphism class at order $24$. Every positive construction has a literal
checked map to its stored class. The sole stored class in turn has a
literal checked map to the source representative, whose graph6 input and
comment line are bound exactly. This proves Theorem~\ref{main}.

The certificate is accompanied by independent implementation controls.
An oracle enumerating five-vertex subsets and their cycles checks the
insertion and rollback code on all $1024$ graphs on five vertices at
three budgets ($15408$ insertion decisions), and on the source graph.
A separate orbit-set computation tests all $31185$ three-colour vectors
on the $21$ exterior models, obtaining $12963$ orbits and checking that
every prefix of each minimum survives. All $24$ rooted versions of the
source graph are explicitly transported to the retained root and
exterior lists; their $384$ attachment insertions pass the actual degree,
symmetry and pentagon-budget tests. These controls supplement, rather
than replace, the coverage proofs. Failed or incomplete executions are
not accepted certificates. The native checker rebuilds the root and
exterior inventories and runs the complete boundary search by default.

